.claude/pregate-*/round*/taste/

# Harness mutants: 24 single-defect copies of a fixture, regenerated deterministically by
# mutate5.mjs. The evidence is the matrix it prints, which IS committed.
.claude/pregate-*/round*/mutants/

# Raw adverse-pass transcripts (public repo). Codex echoes the loaded skill files
# verbatim — personal quotes, home-directory paths, private rule text — so the log
# itself never ships. The dispositioned findings go in the session log instead.
#
# The pattern below was originally scoped to one directory, and six Sol logs were
# committed outside it before anyone noticed — they carried ~450 home-directory
# paths, a 1642-line private skill file echoed by `sed`, and cross-project rollout
# summaries out of ~/.codex/memories. Caught before any push and stripped from the
# unpushed commits. Match the FILENAME anywhere in the repo, not a location, so
# the next evidence directory cannot reopen this.
#
# It reopened anyway, one round later. Naming `SOL-*` was an ENUMERATION of one
# agent, and the class is "raw agent or tool transcript" — four `*-codex.log`
# files from the same fan-out were already committed (unpushed) carrying 23–50
# home-directory paths each, `~/.claude/skills` and `~/.codex/memories`
# references. So the rule became: no `.log` under `.claude/` ships, whoever wrote
# it. That also covers the test-suite captures, which are clean but are 113KB of
# regenerable noise whose numbers belong in the session log anyway.
#
# And it reopened a THIRD time on the very next adverse pass, because `.log` was
# an enumeration too — by file EXTENSION. `SOL-ROUND2.md` is a 794KB, 11485-line
# raw transcript that happens to end in `.md`, and it carried the verbatim `sed`
# dump of a 1642-line private skill file.
#
# Three narrowings in a row is the signal that a glob is the wrong instrument: a
# pattern has to predict the FILENAME, and the thing that actually defines this
# class is the CONTENT. The real gate is now `test/no-private-paths.test.mjs`,
# which fails the suite if any tracked file contains an absolute path into a
# private agent-tooling directory. The patterns below are a convenience so the
# usual suspects never reach the index; they are not the defense.
#
# Evidence that SHOULD ship is prose and screenshots, both of which a human read
# before committing.
#
# NAMING CONVENTION, so the two never collide again: `SOL-ROUND*` is an agent's
# OUTPUT and never ships; a brief I wrote to send TO an agent is `BRIEF-ROUND*`
# and does. The `SOL-ROUND*.md` pattern below correctly refused to commit a
# round-10 brief that was still using the old name — the right fix was to rename
# the brief, NOT to add a negation for `briefs/`. A negation there would let the
# next raw transcript dropped into that directory ship, which is the exact hole
# this rule keeps reopening. If a pattern blocks something you meant to commit,
# rename the file.
.claude/pregate-*/sol/
.claude/**/*.log
.claude/**/SOL-ROUND*.md
.claude/**/SOL-VERDICT*.txt
SOL-*.log
sol-*.log
